---
// /privacy. The legal text lives here, not in site.yaml: it is prose about
// this code, and it has to be edited next to the code it describes
// (functions/api, schema.sql, src/scripts/hit.ts).
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Mark from '../components/Mark.astro';
import Foot from '../components/Foot.astro';
const site = (await getEntry('site', 'site'))!.data;
const updated = 'October 9, 2026';
---
<Base title="Privacy | Wren Automation" description="What Wren Automation collects, why, and how to have it deleted." hue="lavender">
<div class="wrap">
  <nav class="nav" aria-label="Page">
    <a href="/" aria-label="Wren Automation, front page"><Mark /></a>
    <div class="right">
      <button class="theme" type="button" data-theme-toggle data-to-dark={site.theme.to_dark} data-to-light={site.theme.to_light} aria-label="Switch between light and dark">{site.theme.to_dark}</button>
    </div>
  </nav>
</div>
<main>
<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">Wren Automation is one person, William Jin, in Edmonton, Alberta. This page says exactly what this site and my outreach collect. Last updated {updated}.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="s">
  <div class="wrap legal">
    <h2>The form</h2>
    <p>If you fill in the audit form I store what you typed (name, email, phone, what eats your week, your questions) with the page you sent it from, the campaign or referring link that brought you, your country, your IP address and your browser's user-agent string. It goes into a Cloudflare D1 database and I get a notification. I use it to reply to you and to prepare the call. It is never sold, rented, or added to a marketing list.</p>
    <p>The form is checked for bots by Cloudflare Turnstile, under its <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/">privacy addendum</a>.</p>

    <h2>Page views</h2>
    <p>Each page view writes one row: the page, how far down it you scrolled, how long it was visible, whether you clicked through to the form or touched a field, your viewport width, your country, the referring link and your user-agent. No IP address. It also notes a few clicks: the buttons that lead to the form or a booking, starting or sending the form, and how much of a video you watched. And for heatmaps, which part of the page each click lands on and how far down you got, never what you type. Heatmaps only show them as totals per page.</p>
    <p>If you say yes to the cookie, I may also record your visit to the page (where you scroll and click, and what shows on screen) and watch it back to fix the site. Anything you type into a field is masked, and recordings are deleted after 90 days. Sometimes I ask one short question in a box at the bottom of the page. That only happens if you said yes, and your answer is kept with your visits so I can see what brought you here.</p>
    <p>If an email from me had a link, the link carries a short code (<code>?r=</code>) so I know which email brought you. Links I post on social profiles and videos go through <code>/go/</code> and are tagged with the channel. I use this only to see which outreach works. Your country comes from Cloudflare, which reads it from your connection; no cookie holds it. Cloudflare Web Analytics may also be on; it is cookieless and gives me counts, not people.</p>

    <h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
    <p>This site sets two cookies of its own and none from anyone else.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>wv</code>: a random id so your visits to this site join up, and a form you send later is tied to the link that first brought you. It lasts up to 400 days, is never shared, and no ad or tracking company sees it. It is optional.</li>
      <li><code>wc</code>: your answer on the cookie banner, yes or no, for six months, so I don't ask again. It is needed for the site to respect your choice.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In the EU, the UK, Switzerland, Brazil and Quebec, <code>wv</code> is set only after you say yes on the banner. Elsewhere it is on unless you say no. A browser that sends Global Privacy Control is treated as a no everywhere. You can change your answer any time with "Cookie settings" at the bottom of every page; a no deletes <code>wv</code>.</p>
    <p>Your browser also keeps a few settings on your device that never leave it. It remembers the light or dark theme. For the current tab only, it keeps the link you arrived from and where you were on the page.</p>
    <p>Other services load only when you use them. Cloudflare Turnstile checks the form for bots. If you book a call, the booking page is my own. Your name, email and the time go into my Google Calendar, and Google sends the invite with the Meet link. Fonts come from Google Fonts and Fontshare, which see your IP address when they send them.</p>

    <h2>Email I send you</h2>
    <p>I email firms who haven't asked, which is what cold outreach is. Addresses come from public sources: a company's own website and public business listings. Those messages can carry a one-pixel image that tells me the message was opened, and they carry an unsubscribe link. Unsubscribe, or reply with "stop", and the address is suppressed: I will not email it again. Reply once and you are in an ordinary conversation, nothing more.</p>

    <h2>Text messages</h2>
    <p>If you tick the texts box on the form, I store your phone number, that you ticked it, and when. I text you only about your request: replies, scheduling, reminders. Up to 4 texts a month. Reply STOP and you get no more texts; reply HELP for help. Full terms are on the <a href="/terms">terms page</a>.</p>
    <p>No mobile information will be shared with third parties or affiliates for marketing or promotional purposes. Text opt-in data and consent are never shared with any third party. The only company that sees your number is Telnyx, the carrier that delivers the texts.</p>

    <h2>Client work</h2>
    <p>When I build for a client I handle their data under the agreement we sign, and only for that work. Nothing from a client's data ever comes into this site.</p>

    <h2 id="portal">The client portal</h2>
    <p>Clients sign in at app.wrenautomation.com. I store your login email, who on your team has access, and a log of what each person did there. I use it to run your account and nothing else.</p>

    <h2 id="connected-accounts">Accounts you connect</h2>
    <p>In the portal you can connect your own Facebook Page, Instagram, LinkedIn profile or Page, YouTube channel, X, TikTok and Google Business Profile, and your Gmail or Microsoft 365 mailbox. Each one is a normal sign-in on that platform's own page. You see what it asks for and pick the account. I never see your password.</p>
    <p>What each one is for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>YouTube: upload the videos you approve, show you your channel, videos, comments and stats, and post the comment replies you approve.</li>
      <li>Google Business Profile: post the updates and review replies you approve, and show you your listing and reviews.</li>
      <li>LinkedIn: post what you approve to your profile or Page, and show you its comments and follower counts.</li>
      <li>Facebook, Instagram, X and TikTok: post what you approve, show you comments, messages and post stats, and send the replies you approve.</li>
      <li>Gmail and Microsoft 365: send the emails you approve from your own address. If your admin allows it, also read new mail so replies show up in your portal inbox.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Nothing is posted or sent from your accounts without your approval.</p>

    <h2>How I store it</h2>
    <p>The platform gives me a token, not your password. Long-lived tokens are encrypted (AES-256-GCM) and kept in my database under your account only. Short-lived tokens stay in memory. Tokens never appear in logs.</p>
    <p>I also store what I read from your accounts: posts, comments, messages, mail and stats. You see it in the portal, and I use it to draft posts and replies for you to approve. For a draft, I send the text that task needs to an AI model provider: Anthropic, Google (Gemini), Cohere, or others through OpenRouter. They get that text only, never your tokens or logins. None of it is sold, used for ads, or shared with other clients.</p>

    <h2 id="google">Google user data</h2>
    <p>Wren's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs to any other app will adhere to the <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>, including the Limited Use requirements. Google data is used only for the features above, for you. It is never sold, never used for ads, and never used to train AI models. A person reads it only with your permission, for security, or when the law requires it.</p>
    <p>YouTube features use the YouTube API Services. If you connect a channel, the <a href="https://www.youtube.com/t/terms">YouTube Terms of Service</a> and the <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google Privacy Policy</a> apply.</p>

    <h2 id="disconnecting">Disconnecting</h2>
    <p>Press Disconnect next to the account in the portal. Its token and the account record are deleted at once, and I can no longer reach it. You can also remove Wren on the platform itself:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Google and YouTube: <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">your Google account's third-party access page</a>.</li>
      <li>LinkedIn: Settings &amp; Privacy, Data privacy, Permitted services.</li>
      <li>Facebook and Instagram: Settings, Business integrations.</li>
      <li>X: Settings, Security and account access, Apps and sessions.</li>
      <li>TikTok: Settings and privacy, Security, Manage app permissions.</li>
      <li>Microsoft: <a href="https://myapps.microsoft.com">My Apps</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>To have the rest deleted too, see <a href="/data-deletion">data deletion</a>.</p>

    <h2>Who else sees it</h2>
    <p>Cloudflare hosts the site and the database. Google Workspace carries my email. Resend and Discord deliver the notification when the form is submitted. Telnyx delivers texts. Amazon Web Services stores the visit recordings and hosts the client portal and its database. Restate runs the portal's background work. The AI providers above see the text a draft needs. Each of them sees the data it needs to do that and nothing else. No advertising networks, no data brokers.</p>

    <h2>Keeping and deleting</h2>
    <p>Form submissions are kept while there's a reason to: a live conversation, a build, the records a business has to keep. Page-view rows are kept as counts. Email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> and I will tell you what I hold about you, correct it, or delete it. No form, no account, no wait: one email to a person who reads it. For data from a connected account, see <a href="/data-deletion">data deletion</a>.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>If this changes, the date at the top changes with it.</p>
  </div>
</section>
</main>
<Foot site={site} />
</Base>
